The '''2002 UEFA Champions League final''' was the final match of the [[2001–02 UEFA Champions League]], Europe's primary club [[Association football|football]] competition. The show-piece event was contested between [[Bayer 04 Leverkusen|Bayer Leverkusen]] of Germany and [[Real Madrid CF|Real Madrid]] of Spain at [[Hampden Park]] in [[Glasgow]], Scotland,<ref name="hampdenpark">{{cite news |first=Sean |last=Smith |title=Glasgow in party mood |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport1/hi/football/champions_league/1984838.stm |work=BBC Sport |publisher=British Broadcasting Corporation |date=13 May 2002 |access-date=31 December 2010 }}</ref> on 15 May 2002 to decide the winner of the Champions League.<ref name="hampdenpark"/> Leverkusen appeared in the final for the first time, whereas Real Madrid appeared in their 12th final.

Each club needed to progress through two group stages, and two knockout rounds to reach the final. Real Madrid won their group and moved into the second group stage, which they also won, before facing the defending champions [[FC Bayern Munich|Bayern Munich]] and [[FC Barcelona|Barcelona]] in the knockout stage. Bayer Leverkusen finished second in their group behind Barcelona and progressed to the second group stage. There, they won their group, before beating the likes of [[Liverpool F.C.|Liverpool]] and [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] to progress to the final.

Before the match, a minute of silence was held in honour of Ukrainian manager [[Valeriy Lobanovskyi]], who died two days earlier.<ref name="championsleaguefinal">{{cite web |url=http://news.bbc.co.uk/sport2/hi/football/champions_league/1989548.stm |title=Champions League final clockwatch |work=BBC Sport |publisher=BBC |date=15 May 2002 |access-date=31 December 2010}}</ref>

Real Madrid were regarded as favourites before the match and took the lead in the eighth minute through [[Raúl (footballer)|Raúl]]. [[Lúcio]] equalised five minutes later, before [[Zinedine Zidane]] scored the winning goal on the stroke of half-time, a left-footed volley into the top corner that has since gone down as one of the greatest goals in the history of the competition,<ref>{{cite news |title=15 years on from Zidane's final wonder goal |url=https://www.uefa.com/uefachampionsleague/news/0239-0e9c7f74cced-4d02500f3f8e-1000--15-years-on-from-zidane-s-final-wonder-goal/ |website=[[UEFA]] |publisher=Union of European Football Associations |date=15 May 2017 |access-date=23 September 2018 }}</ref> to secure Real Madrid's ninth European Cup.

